/-! # Normal arithmetic models of an unpointed smooth family

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0EZJ, normal approximation and family descent;
Tags 055I and 05F4 for the unpointed geometric fibre condition.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-normal-domain-smooth-family-models)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory Limits Opposite

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A smooth separated finitely presented family over a normal domain has normal
finite type integer models, with the base and family both recovered as scheme limits.

The index set is a set of integer subalgebras, ordered by inclusion. The isomorphisms
`eA` identify the base diagram with their spectra, including all transition and cone maps.
`Over (op i₀)` is precisely the tail of indices greater than or equal to `i₀`.
`baseChangeDiagram D p` has objects `pullback p (D.map j.hom)` and canonical
base-change transitions; `baseChangeCone` has vertex `pullback p (α.app (op i₀))`.
The equality for `e.hom` makes the displayed isomorphism an isomorphism over `Spec R`.
`GeometricallyConnected` uses `ConnectedSpace`, which includes nonemptiness, on every
field-valued base change; equivalently every residue-field fibre remains nonempty and
connected under every field extension. No point or section is chosen.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0EZJ, normal approximation and smooth family descent;
Tags 055I and 05F4 replace the section-component argument.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-normal-domain-smooth-family-models)

Proof:
1. Let $I$ be the set of all integrally closed finitely generated $\mathbf Z$-subalgebras of $R$,
ordered by inclusion, with $A_i=i$. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-normal-finite-type-subalgebra-extension]
to the prime subring to obtain a member of $I$, to the subring generated by the union of two
finite generating sets to obtain a common upper bound, and to the prime subring with one element
$r\in R$ adjoined to obtain a member containing $r$. These applications are legitimate because all
three input subrings are finitely generated. Thus $I$ is nonempty and directed and
$R=\bigcup_iA_i$. Each $A_i$ is a domain as a subring of the domain $R$. It is a set of subsets
with ring operations inherited from $R$, so it is small in a universe containing $R$.
Uses: lem-weil-pbc-normal-finite-type-subalgebra-extension.

2. The inclusions $A_i\subseteq R$ exhibit $R$ as the ring colimit: compatible maps $h_i:A_i\to B$
determine $h(r)=h_i(r)$ for any stage containing $r$; a common upper stage proves independence of
this choice and preservation of sums and products. By [lem-weil-pbc-spec-small-colimit-limit],
$S=\operatorname{Spec}R$ is the scheme limit of $S_i=\operatorname{Spec}A_i$. Every map $S_j\to
S_i$ between these affine schemes is affine by [pqf-lem-affine-local-target], using the one-member
affine cover $\{S_i\}$ of the target, whose preimage $S_j$ is affine; and these affine schemes are
quasi-compact and quasi-separated by [lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs]. Each $S_i$ is of finite
type over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbf Z$, hence Noetherian by
[lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian]. The generic point $(0)$ of $\operatorname{Spec}R$ maps
to $(0)$ of each $\operatorname{Spec}A_i$ because $A_i\to R$ is injective; this point is dense
since $A_i$ is a domain, proving dominance. A tail has the same union: given $A_i$ and $r$, take a
common upper bound of $i$ and a stage containing $r$. Thus the same ring-colimit and
spectrum-limit argument works on every tail.
Uses: lem-weil-pbc-spec-small-colimit-limit, lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence,
lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian, def-weil-affine-spectrum,
lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs, pqf-lem-affine-local-target.

3. Apply clause (1) of [thm-pd-spreading-out] to this nonempty directed affine-transition qcqs
system and to the given finitely presented $f:X\to S$. It gives an index $a$ and a finitely
presented $p_a:Y_a\to S_a$ together with an $S$-isomorphism $X\cong Y_a\times_{S_a}S$. For $b\geq
a$ write $Y_b=Y_a\times_{S_a}S_b$. Base change preserves finite presentation by
[lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence](3); its clause (2) also makes every $Y_b$ qcqs. These are
precisely the objects and cartesian squares used below.
Uses: thm-pd-spreading-out, lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence.

4. Use [lem-sbc-affine-limit-smooth-stage] for $p_a$, its smooth pullback $f$, and the Noetherian
quasi-separated system of Step 2, to get a stage $b\geq a$ where $p_b$ is smooth. Apply
[lem-weil-pbc-descend-separated] to $Y_b\to S_b$ (view the target as the identity $S_b$-scheme);
the base change to $S$ is the separated $f$, and all source, target and base schemes are qcqs by
Step 3. This gives $c\geq b$ with $p_c$ separated. Smoothness persists to this later stage as
follows. Local finite presentation is preserved by [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence](3). A
lifting problem for its base change consists of a thickening $T\subseteq T^{\prime}$ over the new
base and a compatible map $T\to Y_b$. Smoothness of $p_b$, in [def-sbc-smooth-morphisms], supplies
a lift $T^{\prime}\to Y_b$; pair it with the prescribed map $T^{\prime}\to S_c$ to obtain the
required lift into the fibre product. This proves formal smoothness after base change, hence
smoothness of $p_c$. The same lifting argument applies to every later base change of $p_c$.
Uses: lem-sbc-affine-limit-smooth-stage, lem-weil-pbc-descend-separated,
lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence, def-sbc-smooth-morphisms.

5. Let $E=\{s\in S_c:n_{p_c}(s)=1\}$. This is constructible by
[lem-sfb-geometric-connected-fibre-locus-constructible], since $p_c$ is finitely presented and
$S_c$ is Noetherian. By [lem-weil-pbc-geometric-component-count-base-change], $n_{p_c}$ pulls back
along $S\to S_c$ to $n_f$, which is constantly $1$ by the nonempty geometrically connected fibre
hypothesis. Hence the image of $S$ lies in $E$. Apply
[lem-weil-pbc-limit-contained-in-constructible] to get $i_0\geq c$ such that the image of $S_i\to
S_c$ lies in $E$ for every $i\geq i_0$. Apply component-count base change again: every geometric
fibre of $p_i$ has exactly one connected component, so is nonempty and connected. The
algebraic-closure criterion [lem-weil-geometrically-connected-algebraic-closure] then gives
connectedness after every residue-field extension. In particular all fibres are nonempty
geometrically connected. This argument uses no section.
Uses: lem-sfb-geometric-connected-fibre-locus-constructible,
lem-weil-pbc-geometric-component-count-base-change, lem-weil-pbc-limit-contained-in-constructible,
lem-weil-geometrically-connected-algebraic-closure.

6. Put $X_{i_0}=Y_{i_0}$ and $X_i=X_{i_0}\times_{S_{i_0}}S_i$ for $i\geq i_0$. Associating fibre
products by their universal property identifies these with $Y_i$ and gives $X\cong
X_{i_0}\times_{S_{i_0}}S$. Use the canonical projections for all transition maps, so composition
and the displayed cartesian squares follow from the same universal property. Finite presentation
persists by [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence](3), separatedness by
[lem-weil-pbc-separated-base-change], and smoothness by the explicit lifting argument of Step 4.
Step 5 supplies the fibre condition. Each $X_i$ is of finite type over the Noetherian $S_i$, hence
Noetherian by [lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian]; it is quasi-separated by
[lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence](2). Each $X_j\to X_i$ is a base change of the affine
$S_j\to S_i$, hence affine by [lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence](1).
Uses: lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence, lem-weil-pbc-separated-base-change,
lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian, lem-weil-pbc-affine-morphism-permanence.

7. The cone from $X$ is a scheme limit on the tail. Indeed a compatible cone from any scheme $T$
to the $X_i$ has an $i_0$-leg $u:T\to X_{i_0}$, and composition with $X_i\to S_i$ gives a base
cone. Step 2 gives a unique $v:T\to S$ for that base cone. Its $i_0$-component equals the
composite of $u$ with $X_{i_0}\to S_{i_0}$. The fibre-product universal property therefore gives a
unique $w:T\to X_{i_0}\times_{S_{i_0}}S=X$. At each $i$ its components in $X_{i_0}$ and $S_i$
agree with those of the given leg, hence that leg equals the projection of $w$ by uniqueness for
$X_i=X_{i_0}\times_{S_{i_0}}S_i$. This also proves uniqueness of $w$. All diagrams use the small
set $I$ and fibre products of the given stages; enlarging the universe to contain $R$ and $X$
accommodates these choices. No extra datum on the family has been introduced.
Uses: def-pc-fibre-product.
-/
theorem exists_normal_domain_smooth_family_models
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [IsDomain R] [IsIntegrallyClosed R]
    {X : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ Spec (CommRingCat.of R))
    [Smooth f] [IsSeparated f] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f]
    [QuasiCompact f] [QuasiSeparated f] [GeometricallyConnected f] :
    ∃ (I : Set (Subalgebra ℤ R)),
      I.Nonempty ∧ DirectedOn (· ≤ ·) I ∧
      (∀ i : I, IsDomain i.val ∧ IsIntegrallyClosed i.val ∧ i.val.FG ∧
        IsNoetherianRing i.val) ∧
      (∀ r : R, ∃ i : I, r ∈ i.val) ∧
      ∃ (D : (↥I)ᵒᵖ ⥤ Scheme.{u})
        (eA : ∀ i : I, D.obj (op i) ≅ Spec (CommRingCat.of i.val))
        (α : (Functor.const (↥I)ᵒᵖ).obj (Spec (CommRingCat.of R)) ⟶ D),
        (∀ (i j : I) (h : i ≤ j),
          D.map (homOfLE h).op ≫ (eA i).hom =
            (eA j).hom ≫ Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom
              (Subalgebra.inclusion h).toRingHom)) ∧
        (∀ i : I, α.app (op i) ≫ (eA i).hom =
          Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom i.val.val.toRingHom)) ∧
        (∀ i, IsNoetherian (D.obj i) ∧ QuasiSeparatedSpace (D.obj i) ∧
          IsDominant (α.app i)) ∧
        (∀ {i j : (↥I)ᵒᵖ} (a : i ⟶ j), IsAffineHom (D.map a)) ∧
        ∃ (i₀ : I) (X₀ : Scheme.{u}) (p : X₀ ⟶ D.obj (op i₀))
          (e : X ≅ pullback p (α.app (op i₀))),
          e.hom ≫ pullback.snd p (α.app (op i₀)) = f ∧
          Smooth p ∧ IsSeparated p ∧ LocallyOfFinitePresentation p ∧
          QuasiCompact p ∧ QuasiSeparated p ∧ GeometricallyConnected p ∧
          IsNoetherian X₀ ∧ QuasiSeparatedSpace X₀ ∧
          (∀ j : Over (op i₀),
            Smooth (pullback.snd p (D.map j.hom)) ∧
            IsSeparated (pullback.snd p (D.map j.hom)) ∧
            LocallyOfFinitePresentation (pullback.snd p (D.map j.hom)) ∧
            QuasiCompact (pullback.snd p (D.map j.hom)) ∧
            QuasiSeparated (pullback.snd p (D.map j.hom)) ∧
            GeometricallyConnected (pullback.snd p (D.map j.hom)) ∧
            IsNoetherian ((baseChangeDiagram D p).obj j) ∧
            QuasiSeparatedSpace ((baseChangeDiagram D p).obj j)) ∧
          (∀ {j k : Over (op i₀)} (a : j ⟶ k),
            IsAffineHom ((baseChangeDiagram D p).map a)) ∧
          Nonempty (IsLimit ((Cone.mk (Spec (CommRingCat.of R)) α).whisker
            (Over.forget (op i₀)))) ∧
          Nonempty (IsLimit ((baseChangeCone D (Cone.mk _ α) p).extend e.hom)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
